WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.340
We can´t hide it, here at VisualPolitik we
love Asia, and that’s partly because, we

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:23.840
believe that the world’s centre is already
on this continent.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:33.560
But in the last few decades, one country has
stood out of the crowd, and that is China.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:38.090
For its huge size and the speed at which this
country is growing, China deserves to be given

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:41.909
the chief role in this super production that
is changing our planet.

00:00:41.909 --> 00:00:45.260
But wait a second, let’s not rush into this.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.399
China is not perfect, indeed the country has
some pretty major problems.

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:51.399
&nbsp;

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:55.850
(The last 23rd of May 2017, the rating agency
Moody's, one of the three agencies in charge

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:00.170
of rating the solvency and the risk of its
financial products, reduced the rating of

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.469
China´s debt)

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:06.530
And this is the first reduction Moody has
made on China’s debt in 27 years.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.390
Yes, Ok, I know what you are thinking: Moody’s?

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:11.390
Rating agencies in general?

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.270
I mean, they rarely seem to get it right...

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.550
Now that is true, but it’s also one of the
only things that we have to judge this sort

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:18.550
of thing.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:23.160
“Investors still overwhelmingly rely on
Standard &amp; Poor’s, Moody’s and Fitch when

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.550
deciding whether to buy bonds.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:28.750
The three issue more than 95% of global bond
ratings”.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:34.070
The things is, dear viewers, China has faced
two major threats in the last years: less

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:37.140
growth and much, much, more debt.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:43.340
In fact, this has even lead to an important
question: Can China go bankrupt?

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:48.620
(CHINA'S GREAT GROWTH)

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.570
If we had a time machine and the opportunity
to travel to Mao Tse Tung’s China, the country

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:56.760
we would find would be completely different
to the one we find today.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.390
There wouldn’t be any skyscrapers, and malls,
in fact, there wouldn’t really be much around

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.390
at all…

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:10.450
In the 1970s China was mainly an agricultural
country, and it was extremely poor…

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:19.600
But a few years after Mao had died, a man
known as Deng Xiaoping came to power and he

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.160
realized that communism…well, it hadn’t
worked at all.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:28.480
He immediately put into practise a set of
measures that would change China forever…

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.100
These reforms, and the change China has gone
through since, are going to be the subject

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.460
of an upcoming video, so please don’t forget
to subscribe to this channel!

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:49.150
But, for now, it is important to remember
something: Deng Xiaoping was a turning point

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:50.560
for the country.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.870
(Since then, China has undergone the greatest
economical revolution in the history of mankind.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:03:00.120
In the last three decades, China has managed
to take 500 million people out of poverty

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.520
and to become the largest industrial and trading
power on the planet).

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.870
In other words, if we ask any Chinese person
about his or her living standards, the answer

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:18.660
will be that now it is infinitely better that
it was before.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.690
And don't get me wrong, there is still a lot
of poverty in China, but compared to how it

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.740
was, it’s a whole lot better today.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:30.000
But, as I was saying earlier, it’s not all
roses, and there are some serious clouds on

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.069
the horizon.

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:36.390
(A SYSTEM ADDICTED TO DEBT)

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:41.540
Have a quick look at this chart which shows
what happened to China’s debt in the last

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.290
few years.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:49.060
As you can see it goes up and up and up…

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.239
That is a seriously steep line…

00:03:51.239 --> 00:03:54.940
On the other hand, look closely at what has
happened to Chinese economic growth.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:59.730
As you can see, this chart is headed in the
opposite direction.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:07.400
So, in other words, in China, the economy
is declining and the debt won't stop rising.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:16.910
Look, in gross terms, the Chinese debt has
multiplied by four since 2007.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:22.870
(The Chinese debt equals now more than 260%
of the GDP and many analysts consider this

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.740
number to be especially high for a country
that is yet to become one of the richest in

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:27.740
the world).

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:33.160
Well, most of the debt is actually the responsibility
of Chinese companies.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.160
&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:41.840
Well, the thing is, there is a silver lining,
but there is also a significant negative side:

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:47.370
All that international expansion needs money,
and a great deal of it.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:51.230
And while much of the debt has financed the
expansion of Chinese companies, they have

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.660
also paid for this kind of nonsense:

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:59.220
“Throughout China, there are hundreds of
cities that have almost everything one needs

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.820
for a modern, urban lifestyle: high-rise apartment
complexes, developed waterfronts, skyscrapers,

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:05.370
and even public art.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.590
Everything, that is, except one major factor:
people”

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:13.810
So you can get an idea of the size of all
of that has been built in China in the last

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:18.520
few years, pay attention to the following
number: in just three years, China has used

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:24.020
more concrete than the United States in the
entire 20th Century:

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:29.370
And as I said, everything, absolutely everything,
has been paid for with debt.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.960
So now, we come to a really important question:
What the hell has happened in this country

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.820
to cause it to get into so much debt?

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:42.551
(THE CHINESE GOVERNMENT’S FORWARD FLIGHT)

00:05:42.551 --> 00:05:50.260
For the last few decades, if you could say
the Chinese people were good at one thing

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:53.160
it would be saving money...

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:59.360
Especially the oldest Chinese citizens, they
work and save, that is all they do.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.970
So, thanks to all that effort, China has had
high investment rates.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:07.750
But don’t be fooled, what has happened in
the last years has nothing to do with this.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:11.870
In order to know what has happened, we need
to go back to 2008.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:16.330
At that time, all developed countries were
going through a major financial crisis, and

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:22.300
you can imagine how this affected China's
exports, which are the engine that keeps driving

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.060
China’s forward.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:28.400
The truth is that, at that time, the Chinese
government was terrified that the country

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.430
would be affected by the international crisis.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:35.940
So, in order to prevent it, they came up with
an idea: the economic system had to be transformed,

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.420
and they had to bet on domestic demand.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:43.520
And when it comes from a politician, that
“domestic demand” thing almost always

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.940
means &nbsp;they are planning to start overspending.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.240
And, well, that is exactly what Beijing did...

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:54.700
(In 2008, the Chinese government started a
colossal programme of extra public spending

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:59.380
of 4 trillion yuans, around 600 billion dollars,
meant to be used for building all kinds of

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:04.990
infrastructure: high speed trains, undergrounds,
highways, houses, offices…)

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:07.740
But they went further.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:11.780
The Chinese government thought that government
overspending wasn't enough, so they had to

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:14.590
encourage the companies to do the same.

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:15.910
Well, how could they do it?

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:19.160
Well, they decided to use a traditional remedy:

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:25.030
(In 2008, China also started one of the most
expansive monetary policies ever known.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:29.300
The authorities lowered interest rates and
banks gave money loans to everyone who asked

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.300
for them.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.240
They practically established an open bar of
cheap loans)

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.539
And as always happens, they got completely
drunk.

00:07:34.539 --> 00:07:38.190
In just three years, the price of housing
was an average of 2.4 times more expensive.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:43.639
So, an apartment which cost $100,000 three
years before, would now be worth $240,000.

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:47.610
And, the result?

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:52.330
Companies started to make all kinds of investments:
they built ghost towns, bought out companies

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:55.830
all over the world, and engaged in all kinds
of projects.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.990
They took on whatever crazy risky projects
they felt like!

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:02.180
After all, with the Central Bank was almost
giving the money away…

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:07.190
“Overproduction in key industries is damaging
both China's economic sustainability and foreign

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:11.110
trade relations”
Obviously, many of these projects have never

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:14.410
been profitable, so the companies can’t
give the money back.

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.120
So what do they do then?

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:21.729
Well, they ask for new loans to pay back the
old ones, and the snowball keeps rolling and

00:08:21.729 --> 00:08:23.530
growing bigger and bigger.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:26.840
And this is how the Chinese economy became
addicted to debt.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.900
And now I’ve probably got an idea of you
are thinking…

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:32.490
Those stupid private companies, right?

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.219
Well, not exactly.

00:08:34.219 --> 00:08:39.579
“More than half of the bank debt in China
consists of loans from state-owned banks to

00:08:39.579 --> 00:08:41.690
state-owned enterprises”.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:47.060
And wait a second because there, ohh, there
is much, much more…

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:51.890
(CHINESE BANKS: A WEAPON OF MASS DESTRUCTION)

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:57.560
We’ve already seen how China is currently
going through a debt overdose, but, if this

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:05.029
wasn't enough, there is another danger hiding
in the bushes: a bank in the shadows.

00:09:05.029 --> 00:09:08.290
Banks face certain limitations on the amount
of money they can loan.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:13.160
But Chinese banks have managed to skip these
controls and, in a way, these banks have copied

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:16.440
what the American banks did with their mortgages.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.380
Let me explain: instead of just giving loans,
they set up off-balance companies that issued

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:23.611
debt that banks bought.

00:09:23.611 --> 00:09:27.640
And that is the money they lent, but… surprise
surprise, in the bank’s accounts these aren’t

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:31.990
listed as loans, but as other investments.

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:33.480
A pretty smart workaround, right?

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:38.330
Well, it is estimated that Chinese banks have
hidden more than $2 trillion.

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:43.930
And it has a straightforward consequence:
Chinese banks can be way less solvent that

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:45.620
their official numbers might indicate.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:51.940
And, besides, much of this debt is opaque,
so the actual debt in China could be much

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:52.940
higher.

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:56.930
(“The fast spread of off-balance activities
and bank investment actually means risk of

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:02.170
hidden loans which could threaten China’s
financial security” Shang Fulin, Ex-President

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:04.300
of China’s Banking Regulatory Commision.)

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.230
And wait, because there’s more:

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:12.490
“A growing number of nonbank financial firms
also market their own wealth management products

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:15.810
and invest the money with little disclosure
of where the money goes”.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:20.750
That’s right, in the last few years, those
companies which get money from people to make

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:25.240
investments that are risky and opaque have
become more and more popular.

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:26.620
And why is that?

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:31.320
Well, of course, that’s in exchange for
higher rates of return.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:35.570
The thing is this craving for loans can cause
quite a bit of indigestion.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:40.880
This is a risk and the government has admitted
that it has tried to limit company loans.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:46.100
But since there are a lot of unsold ghost
towns, empty houses, and bankrupt real estate

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:50.630
agencies, the government told the banks in
the last few years to give away loans as if

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:55.310
there were no tomorrow, because the family
debt level is still so low…

00:10:55.310 --> 00:11:01.730
“The Fed economists note that China's mortgage
loans increased by 35% in 2016”.

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:06.589
And now, dear viewer, you might know why some
analysts are warning that if they don´t stop

00:11:06.589 --> 00:11:11.840
this debt vortex China could become a new
Japan, a country that has been in crisis since

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:12.840
the 80s.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:19.230
And there is still more, now we know why Xi
Jinping and China need globalization so much

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:26.110
- their companies need foreign markets to
deal with all the debt they have issued.

00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:30.510
But not everything is doomed and the future
is yet to come.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:37.110
In China, the saving rate is still high, enough
for foreign capital to arrive in the country.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:42.160
But…there a risk, and Moody’s has given
the first warning.

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:46.370
Now, it is time for the government to start
working to make reforms…

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:48.100
And now we’d like to know your thoughts.

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:52.230
You can tell us what you think in this survey
as well as the comments.

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.330
What do you think will happen in China?

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:57.790
Do you think stagnation will turn it into
a new Japan?

00:11:57.790 --> 00:12:01.410
Or will we see new reforms to open China even
wider to the west?

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:06.370
So, I really hope you enjoyed that video,
please hit like if you did, and don’t forget

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:11.010
to subscribe if you haven’t already, brand
new videos every Monday and Thursday.

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:15.490
And don’t forget to check out the Reconsider
Media podcasts - they provided the vocals

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:17.810
in this episode that aren’t mind.

00:12:17.810 --> 00:12:20.050
And as always, I’ll see you in the next
video.

